"At the beginning of the surah, the letters \"Ha-Mim\" and the oath sworn by the clear Book demonstrate that the Quran needs no other proof to prove itself. The origin of the Book, the Preserved Tablet (Lawh al-Mahfuz) or Allah's knowledge, is exalted and full of wisdom. Despite the transgression of the polytheists, Allah reminds that He will not abandon the warning, that He also sent prophets to earlier nations, and that they were destroyed.\n\nThe confession of the polytheists when asked that Allah is the Creator of the heavens and the earth demonstrates His power. Just as Allah revives the dead earth, it is stated that they will also be brought forth into a new life through the Quran. Allah, who created all the pairs, teaches them to mount their riding beasts and give thanks; the importance of supplication (du'a) and glorification (tasbih) during travel is emphasized.\n\nThe polytheists ascribe a portion of His servants to Allah (through incarnation [hulul], offspring, or association of partners). Yet, not considering everything created by Allah as belonging to Him and associating partners with Him is ingratitude. Then, the claim that Allah has taken daughters is rejected; their regarding the angels as female and their fatalistic excuses are criticized. Their blind imitation of their fathers' religion is condemned, and Abraham's heritage of monotheism (tawhid) is recalled.\n\nIn response to the Quraysh's assertion that the Quran ought to have been sent down to a great man from the two cities, it is declared that it is Allah who distributes mercy, that worldly wealth is transient, and that the Hereafter is for the righteous. A devil is assigned to whoever turns away from the remembrance of the Most Merciful (al-Rahman); on the Day of Resurrection, friends become enemies.\n\nThe sending of Moses (Musa) to Pharaoh and his miracles are recounted; Pharaoh's arrogance and the obedience of his people cause their destruction. This narrative serves as a comfort to the Prophet and a warning to the Quraysh. Then, the example of Jesus is presented; it is emphasized that he is not a deity, but a servant and a prophet. The fact that Jesus is a sign of the Hour is stated, and the command to follow his path is given.\n\nIn the final section of the surah, the promise of Paradise to the righteous, the punishment of Hell for the criminals, and the matter of intercession (shafa'ah) are addressed. It is stated that Allah knows everything, that idols cannot intercede, and that only those who knowingly bear witness to the truth can intercede. The polytheists are criticized for acknowledging Allah's role as the Creator and yet still turning away from the truth.\n\nThe Prophet (peace be upon him) submitted to his Lord that his people did not believe, and Allah informed him to pay no heed to them for now and to turn away saying, 'Peace be upon you,' for they will soon come to know.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, it will be said addressing the righteous servants: 'Today there is no fear for you.' They will be received with joy on their faces, and hospitality will be served to them in golden platters in Paradise. Therein is whatever souls desire and eyes delight in, and they will abide therein eternally.\n\nThe criminals, however, are in the torment of Hell. They cry out, 'O Malik,' asking their Lord to put an end to them. But they are told that they are to remain permanently and that there is no salvation through death. For they did not love the truth sent to them in the worldly life.\n\nAllah questions whether the polytheists have devised a firm plot against the Prophet. Yet He hears their secret talks, and beside them are angels recording everything. Allah's plot is firmer.\n\nFor the sake of the Prophet's supplication, 'O Lord,' it is emphasized that the Most Merciful has no offspring. The polytheists do not believe. Finally, the Prophet is commanded to pay no heed to them and to pass them by with peace; they will soon come to know. With this warning, Surah az-Zukhruf ends and Surah ad-Dukhan begins."